notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T22:10:54.583Z","harness:claude-code","First composite baseline for this entitlement, measured 2026-07-27 on the shipped 2026.7.26 build against this repository's own 2,113-item tracker. The acceptance criteria ask for tokens per completed task tracked release over release and no baseline had been recorded, so this is the number to move.\n\nOutput bytes, human-readable default format, one invocation each. Token estimates are bytes divided by four.\n\n  contracts --summary                4,193 B    ~1.0k tok\n  context --limit 10                 6,330 B    ~1.6k tok\n  next --limit 5                     8,887 B    ~2.2k tok\n  get <item> (default depth)         4,532 B    ~1.1k tok\n  get <item> --depth brief           4,491 B    ~1.1k tok\n  list-open --limit 20               4,517 B    ~1.1k tok\n  search <phrase> --limit 5          1,970 B    ~0.5k tok\n  deps <item> --format context       6,486 B    ~1.6k tok\n  graph impact <item> --summary        229 B    ~0.06k tok\n  stats                                439 B    ~0.1k tok\n  validate                          22,407 B    ~5.6k tok\n  root help                         13,542 B    ~3.4k tok\n  create --help                      7,818 B    ~2.0k tok\n  update --help                      8,261 B    ~2.1k tok\n\nComposite for the canonical orientation loop an agent runs before it has done any work — contracts summary, context, next, then reading the one item it picked — is 23,942 B, roughly 6.0k tokens. That is the floor, and it assumes the agent already knows the command names. An agent that reads root help first pays 13,542 B more, and one that reads the help for the two commands it will mutate with pays another 16,079 B, which takes a cold start to roughly 13.4k tokens before a single useful mutation.\n\nThree observations the individual component items do not state.\n\nOne. Diagnostics dominate. A single pm validate costs more than the entire orientation loop, and it is the command an agent is most likely to run when something is already wrong, which is exactly when its remaining budget is smallest. This is the composite evidence for pm-cha95z and it is stronger than the per-command case: the diagnostic is not merely large, it is larger than everything the agent did to get to the point of needing it.\n\nTwo. Help is a third of the cold start and is governed by nothing. The command and flag surfaces have owners and budgets, and the help rendered from them does not.\n\nThree. The depth ladder does not help a leaf. On a Story with six dependencies and no children, brief and default differ by 41 bytes, under one percent. The ladder is sized by children, so the reduction an agent expects from asking for less does not arrive on the items it most often reads. That is not a defect in the ladder, it is evidence that depth is the wrong axis for the common case and that the intent-scoped projections on pm-cxr0jb are the mechanism that would actually pay here.\n\nRe-measure this block each time a component lands and record the delta, since the point of this entitlement is that a component win that raises the composite is a loss."
body: ""
